<!-- 4. FLOWCHART SYMBOLS -->
<section>
    <h3><span class="keyword" data-def="Flowchart: diagram that represents an algorithm using standard symbols and flow lines.">Flowcharts</span>: Standard Symbols</h3>
    <div class="question-box">If every diagram uses different shapes, how can anyone mark it fairly?</div>
    <div class="box">
        <p class="fragment"><strong>Terminator (oval):</strong> Start/End</p>
        <p class="fragment"><strong>I/O (parallelogram):</strong> Input or Output</p>
        <p class="fragment"><strong>Process (rectangle):</strong> Calculation / assignment</p>
        <p class="fragment"><strong>Decision (diamond):</strong> Branching (Yes/No or True/False)</p>
        <p class="fragment"><strong>Connector (small circle):</strong> Continue flow elsewhere</p>
    </div>
    <div class="box-blue fragment">
        <p class="small-text"><strong>Marking tip:</strong> decision branches must be labelled and flowlines must connect clearly.</p>
    </div>
    <section data-background="#fff3e0">
        <h3>🧠 Quick Check</h3>
        <div class="question-box">Which flowchart symbol represents a decision?</div>
        <div class="fragment box-blue">
            <p><strong>Diamond</strong></p>
        </div>
    </section>
</section>

<!-- 5. TRACE TABLES -->
<section>
    <h3><span class="keyword" data-def="Trace table: table used to track variable values and outputs step-by-step through an algorithm.">Trace Tables</span></h3>
    <div class="question-box">How do you prove your algorithm does what you think it does?</div>
    <div class="cols">
        <div class="box">
            <p class="fragment">A trace table records variable values after each step.</p>
            <p class="fragment">It helps verify logic and spot incorrect branches.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="box-blue">
            <h4><i class="fa-solid fa-pen-to-square"></i> Top tip</h4>
            <p class="small-text fragment">Write the value after <em>every</em> assignment, even if it didn’t change.</p>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

<!-- 6. MOD + DIV -->
<section>
    <h3>MOD &amp; DIV</h3>
    <div class="question-box">How do we express remainder and integer division clearly in algorithms?</div>
    <div class="cols">
        <div class="box">
            <p class="fragment"><span class="keyword" data-def="MOD: returns the remainder after integer division.">MOD</span> returns the remainder.</p>
            <p class="fragment"><span class="keyword" data-def="DIV: integer division returning the quotient without remainder.">DIV</span> returns the integer quotient.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="box-blue">
            <p class="small-text fragment">Example: <code>17 MOD 5 = 2</code></p>
            <p class="small-text fragment">Example: <code>17 DIV 5 = 3</code></p>
        </div>
    </div>
    <section data-background="#fff3e0">
        <h3>🧠 Quick Check</h3>
        <div class="question-box">What does <code>10 MOD 3</code> evaluate to?</div>
        <div class="fragment box-blue">
            <p><strong>1</strong> — remainder after dividing 10 by 3.</p>
        </div>
    </section>
</section>

<!-- 7. PSEUDOCODE BASICS -->
<section>
    <h3><span class="keyword" data-def="Pseudocode: language-agnostic description of an algorithm using structured, readable statements.">Pseudocode</span> Basics</h3>
    <div class="question-box">How can we write algorithms so humans understand them, regardless of language?</div>
    <div class="cols">
        <div class="box">
            <p class="fragment"><strong>Assignment:</strong> <code>x ← 5</code></p>
            <p class="fragment"><strong>Selection:</strong> IF / ELSE</p>
            <p class="fragment"><strong>Iteration:</strong> FOR / WHILE</p>
            <p class="fragment"><strong>I/O:</strong> INPUT / OUTPUT</p>
        </div>
        <div class="box-blue">
            <h4><i class="fa-solid fa-code"></i> Example</h4>
            <pre class="fragment"><code>INPUT n\nIF n MOD 2 = 0 THEN\n    OUTPUT \"even\"\nELSE\n    OUTPUT \"odd\"\nEND IF</code></pre>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

<!-- 8. MISCONCEPTION CHECK -->
<section>
    <section>
        <h2><i class="fa-solid fa-check-double"></i> Misconception Check</h2>
        <div class="box">
            <p class="fragment"><strong>A.</strong> Unlabelled decision branches are acceptable if the meaning is “obvious”.</p>
            <p class="fragment"><strong>B.</strong> Trace tables can help verify an algorithm’s logic.</p>
            <p class="fragment"><strong>C.</strong> MOD returns an integer quotient.</p>
            <p class="fragment"><strong>D.</strong> Pseudocode is intended to be readable and language-agnostic.</p>
        </div>
        <p class="small-text" style="text-align:center; margin-top:0.6em;">
            <i class="fa-solid fa-arrow-down blue"></i> <span class="blue">Press Down</span> for answers
        </p>
    </section>

    <section data-background="#f4f6f8">
        <h3>Answers</h3>
        <div class="box">
            <p class="fragment"><strong>A: False.</strong> Labels reduce ambiguity and are required for clear marking.</p>
            <p class="fragment"><strong>B: True.</strong> They track variable states step-by-step.</p>
            <p class="fragment"><strong>C: False.</strong> MOD returns the remainder; DIV returns the quotient.</p>
            <p class="fragment"><strong>D: True.</strong> It focuses on logic, not language syntax.</p>
        </div>
    </section>
</section>

